2025-05-02 17:22:48,353 - __main__ - INFO - Displaying memory: {'id': '10572ff2-009d-4b63-ae4b-86aa48808024', 'score': 2.03417706489563, 'content': "Now make it red instead Sure! Here's another example of how you can modify the prompt to include colors:\n\nGiven prompt: Select one color from the dropdown list below and select the corresponding element(s) in the design.\n\nDesired output:\n\n1. Red - This is a bold, dramatic color that instantly draws attention. Use it as the primary color, or mix with other bold hues such as orange or yellow for contrast. Add some subtle shading or gradient effects to create depth and interest.\n\n2. Green - Green is a versatile choice that works well on most surfaces. It lends itself to earthy textures and natural scenes, but also looks great when used sparingly to accentuate architectural features. Consider using green leaves or grasses for a pop of texture.\n\n3. Blue - Blue has always been a favorite color among artists and designers because of its wide range of moods and emotions. It can be used in monochromatic designs, or combined with other blues to create deeper tones or variations. Choose a cool blue or warm blue for the base color, then use contrasting hues or patterns to add dimension and interest.\n\n4. Yellow - Yellow adds an infectious energy to any design, making it a popular choice for brighten up a room. Use it as a primary color, or play around with various shades and tints to find your own unique effect. Mix yellow with other warm hues such as orange or pink for added contrast and interest.\n\n5. Purple - If you want to take a different approach, try purple. This deep, royal color is often associated with luxury and sophistication, so it can create a sense of elegance and refinement in a design. Use it as a secondary color, or pair it with other purples for contrast and interest. Add some metallic highlights or opaque gradients to give the impression of shimmering surface textures.\n\nRemember to keep the overall look and feel of the design in mind while selecting colors. Remember, it's best to start small and gradually build upon those choices over time until you have something that feels cohesive and visually appealing. Good luck!", 'image_path': 'output/images/081483a3-d6b7-417a-abd5-4872ae96f534.png', 'model_path': 'output/models/e5aa4fac-f2f3-4bc4-86d5-6907dcdc2754.glb', 'timestamp': '2025-05-02 17:21:37', 'original_prompt': 'Now make it red instead'}
